  <p>
    <span data-field="location">{{ location }}</span>. — The <span data-field="museum_name">{{ museum_name }}</span> is pleased to present an exhibition that celebrates the life and legacy of Oregon artist <span data-field="artist_name">{{ artist_name }}</span> (<span data-field="artist_dates">{{ artist_dates }}</span>). Organized by <span data-field="organizer_name">{{ organizer_name }}</span> and senior faculty curator <span data-field="curator_name">{{ curator_name }}</span>, the exhibition features a wide range of Stahl's artwork from the past <span data-field="years_active">{{ years_active }}</span> years, including paintings, assemblages, sculpture, and works on paper drawn from private collections as well as the Hallie Ford Museum of Art's permanent collection. The exhibition "John R. Stahl: Oregon Artist" opens <span data-field="opening_date">{{ opening_date }}</span> and continues through <span data-field="closing_date">{{ closing_date }}</span> in the Study Gallery and Print Study Center.
  </p>

  <p>
    Hull says, "From childhood on, Stahl aligned with nature—in its terrain, waterways and rhythms. He was a modern artist interested in pattern and abstract forms, and he was also a traditional artist who created realistic images of birds and landscape. He investigated both these aesthetic realms throughout his long life as an Oregon artist with the result that his art appeals to a broad and varied audience."
  </p>

  <p>
    In addition to being an artist, Stahl was an educator, and environmental activist who was born and educated in <span data-field="birth_location">{{ birth_location }}</span>. Stahl and his wife Janet moved to <span data-field="relocation_city">{{ relocation_city }}</span> in <span data-field="relocation_year">{{ relocation_year }}</span> where he enrolled in the Museum Art School (now the Pacific Northwest College of Art).
  </p>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <p>
    Stahl's instructors at the Museum School included midcentury Northwest artists <span data-field="instructor_1">{{ instructor_1 }}</span>, <span data-field="instructor_2">{{ instructor_2 }}</span>, <span data-field="instructor_3">{{ instructor_3 }}</span>, <span data-field="instructor_4">{{ instructor_4 }}</span>, and <span data-field="instructor_5">{{ instructor_5 }}</span>. He came to be recognized as a younger member of this cohort while also identifying with artists who had been his classmates such as <span data-field="classmate_1">{{ classmate_1 }}</span> and <span data-field="classmate_2">{{ classmate_2 }}</span>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Attracted by the beauty of the Oregon Coast, the Stahl built a house in <span data-field="house_year">{{ house_year }}</span> on Nerats Bay near Tillamook and settled there permanently. Stahl continued to draw artistic inspiration from the landscape and nature throughout the rest of his career.
  </p>

  <p>
    A complimentary lecture will be presented by <span data-field="lecturer_name">{{ lecturer_name }}</span> on <span data-field="lecture_date">{{ lecture_date }}</span> at <span data-field="lecture_time">{{ lecture_time }}</span> in the Roger Hull Lecture Hall at the museum and will further explore the art and life of Stahl.
  </p>

  <p>
    The exhibition is also presented in conjunction with the publication of a full-color, 136 page hardcover book on Stahl's art, life and career, written by Hull with additional short essays by Janet Stahl, former Portland Art Museum curator Bonnie Laing-Malcolmson, and Portland artist Lucinda Parker. The book will be available at the museum for <span data-field="purchase_price">{{ purchase_price }}</span> starting <span data-field="book_availability_date">{{ book_availability_date }}</span>.
  </p>

  <div class="divider"></div>

  <h3>Financial Support</h3>
  <p>
    Financial support for this exhibition and accompanying book was provided by generous contributions from numerous private individuals, as well as by general operating support grants from the City of Salem's Transient Occupancy Tax funds and the Oregon Arts Commission.
  </p>

  <h3>ADDITIONAL INFORMATION</h3>
  <h4>About the Hallie Ford Museum of Art</h4>
  <p>
    Oregon's third largest art museum features works by Pacific Northwest and Native American artists, and includes a diverse collection of traditional European, American and Asian art, as well as artifacts that date from antiquity. Frequently changing exhibitions include lectures, special events, tours, artist demonstrations and educational opportunities for children and adults.
  </p>

  <p>
    The museum is located at <span data-field="museum_address">{{ museum_address }}</span>. Hours are Tuesday through Saturday from <span data-field="hours_morning">{{ hours_morning }}</span> to <span data-field="hours_evening">{{ hours_evening }}</span>. The galleries are closed on Monday. General admission is <span data-field="general_admission_price">{{ general_admission_price }}</span> for seniors and <span data-field="student_admission_price">{{ student_admission_price }}</span> for students 18 and older. Students 17 and under and children are admitted free. Admission is free for everyone on Tuesdays. For more information call <span data-field="museum_phone_2">{{ museum_phone_2 }}</span> or visit <span data-field="museum_website_2">{{ museum_website_2 }}</span>.
  </p>
